From aec84449c6c82abf3ce80ae81951d5d221585235 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:56:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] feat(controlplane): publish outbox events from the
 application

The outbox only stored events: nothing ran the dispatcher, so the
events written with resource changes were never delivered.

The application lifespan now runs the dispatcher with a pluggable
publisher (create_app(event_publisher=...) for embedding and tests).
By default each event is logged as one JSON line on the
forgemesh.events logger. uvicorn only configures its own loggers, so
when logging is not configured that logger writes to stdout and the
events stay visible.

FORGEMESH_EVENT_WEBHOOK_URL switches to a webhook publisher that POSTs
the envelope with an X-Forgemesh-Event-Id header and an optional bearer
token from FORGEMESH_EVENT_WEBHOOK_TOKEN. Only 2xx counts as delivered;
redirects are not followed, so the token never goes to another address.
FORGEMESH_EVENT_WEBHOOK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS bounds each attempt (default 5,
at most 60). Failures go through the existing retry policy.

The settings are validated at startup and invalid values refuse to
start before the database is opened, like the auth and launcher
settings. Logs, errors and last_error only name the webhook origin:
the path and query may carry a signature, and the token is never
printed; a token sent over plain http to a non-loopback host is warned
about.

The dispatcher starts right after the registry opens, so events written
during deployment recovery are published as well, and stops after the
controller, just before the registry closes. Events still pending at
shutdown are published after the next start.
